{"title":"The Battle of Lundy's Lane (LECTURE ON THE GO)","startsAt":"2026-11-14T12:00:00-05:00","endsAt":"2026-11-14T14:00:00-05:00","status":"ACTIVE","type":"PHYSICAL","description":"**The Toronto History Walks Explorer Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours, email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or for signing up!**\n\nWe have an amazing \"Lecture on the Go\" series for 2026-2027. New topics and fascinating stories abound! Join me, and uncover the world and keep active, warm and cool during the winter and summer months!\n\nI started this walking classroom when we were under that horrible cloud called Covid. This series was popular and helped people get back into society and life.\n\nThe \"Lecture on the Go\" Series is open to members who have purchased the YEARLY MEMBERSHIP PASS, it's free for those members. If you wish to attend a lecture and you are not a yearly pass member then the price **per** lecture is $20.00.\n\nThe Battle of Lundy’s Lane was one of the largest and bloodiest battles fought during the War of 1812. It took place on July 25, 1814, near Niagara Falls. American forces had crossed into Upper Canada (Ontario) and were pushing north along the Niagara Peninsula. British troops, Canadian militia, and Indigenous allies moved to stop them, and the two armies met near a small country lane known as Lundy’s Lane.\n\nThe fierce battle lasted well into the night, with soldiers often fighting in darkness, and confusion.\nOne of the most important points in the battle was a hill where British artillery had been placed. Both sides recognized its importance and launched repeated attacks. It was incredibly intense, with troops charging, and retreating, again and again. Officers on both sides were wounded, and casualties mounted. By the time the battle ended, neither army had achieved a clear victory, but the Americans eventually withdrew from the area, leaving the British in control of the field. It was a costly struggle, and thousands were killed or missing.\n\nToday, the Battle of Lundy’s Lane is remembered as a turning point in the Niagara campaign and one of the defining battles of the war. The battle demonstrated the determination of the Canadians who fought to defend this country.\n\nAlthough the war itself ended without major territorial changes, Lundy’s Lane became an important symbol of resilience. Visitors can still explore the historic battlefield in Niagara Falls and learn about the dramatic events that unfolded there more than two centuries ago, and made the country we call Canada. If you like history and Canadian history at that, or want to learn so much more then this lecture is for you.\n\nUpcoming topics in the 2026 series:\n\n\\*Philosophy 1: Great Minds of the Western World: Goethe to Kant! - (December 2026)\n\\*Philosophy 2: Great Minds of the Eastern\nWorld: Chuang Tzu to Dalai Lama - (December 2026)\nLegacy of Triangle: How a New York Factory Scandal changed North America - (January 2027)\n\nSo book your plans now!\n\nThis event is for all levels and walkers, there are a few steps here and there to navigate, but mostly on one level. This event in a **non-walking** event.\n\nThe lecture begins on the steps of **Old City Hall** at the northeast corner of Bay Street and Queen Street West, and\nhas a ticket price of $20.00 per person, but **free with the yearly membership pass**.\n\n**Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**","venue":{"name":"Toronto Old City Hall","address":"60 Queen St W, Toronto","lat":43.652008,"lng":-79.38153,"city":"Toronto"},"fee":{"amount":20,"currency":"CAD"},"group":"Toronto History Walks","image":"https://secure.meetupstatic.com/photos/event/a/d/8/4/highres_518024420.jpeg"}